Goldman Sachs Strategists Bullish on Global Equities, See Low Recession Risk
</h2>

<h4 class="meta">


<a class="head" href="https://laohu8.com/wemedia/1039043262">


<div class="h-thumb" style="background-image:url(https://community-static.tradeup.com/news/8296859682db4b478146245e72de1922);background-size:cover;"></div>

<div class="h-content">
<p class="h-name">Deep News </p>
<p class="h-time">2025-09-29 20:02</p>
</div>

</a>


</h4>

</header>
<article>
<p>Goldman Sachs strategists indicated that global equity markets are positioned to extend their rally through year-end, supported by resilient U.S. economic performance, underpinning valuations, and the Federal Reserve's dovish policy shift.</p>
<p>The strategy team, including Christian Mueller-Glissmann, upgraded their three-month equity allocation rating to "overweight." They noted that during the slowdown phase of late economic cycles, equity assets typically perform well when policy support remains robust.</p>
<p>"Strong earnings growth, Fed easing without recession, and global fiscal accommodation will continue to support equity markets. Given manageable recession risks, we recommend buying equity market dips before year-end," the team wrote in their report.</p>
<p>In the near term, they downgraded credit assets from "neutral" to "underweight" while maintaining their 12-month "bullish" recommendation on equities. Although equity valuations may exceed current levels, this factor poses constraints for credit assets. However, the strategist team noted that their bearish sentiment toward 12-month credit assets has diminished, considering relatively low recession risks and favorable supply-demand dynamics.</p>
<p>Currently, market optimism about the Fed's timely rate cut initiation to avoid recession has driven global equity markets to historic highs. Additionally, renewed enthusiasm in artificial intelligence has boosted technology giant stock prices, prompting numerous U.S. forecasting institutions to raise their S&P 500 target levels.</p>
<p>Earlier this month, Goldman Sachs' U.S. strategy team also raised their S&P 500 target, expecting the index to climb another 2% over the next three months to reach 6,800 points.</p>
<p>However, as the U.S. labor market begins cooling, market attention will shift to the next corporate earnings season for clues regarding global tariff policy impacts. Data compiled by Bloomberg Intelligence shows analysts expect S&P 500 component companies' third-quarter earnings to grow 7.1% year-over-year, representing the smallest increase in two years.</p>
<p>The Goldman Sachs strategy team also warned of near-term risks from below-expectation economic growth or interest rate volatility. They maintain "neutral" ratings across regional markets and reiterate their preference for diversified investment through international asset allocation.</p>

